## Theorem Proving in a Makefile??
<hr class="section-rule lvl-2">

Realistically it's rare that you are sitting around and wondering whether you can prove Euclid[^1] or Gödel[^2] by using some wild [matrioshka doll]({{_}}/matrioshka) of a Makefile.  But then again, people do get up to all kinds of weird things. 🤔

!!! danger "Caveat"
    **Note that this demo is big,** and the lean/lake/mathlib toolchain here takes significant time to build, and the image on disk afterwards is ~10gb. If you're not interested in running the [notebooking demo]({{_}}/demos/notebooking) later then you may not want to run this one.  

For the purposes of this demo, we'll use `compose.mk` to take the [Lean4 prover](https://leanprover-community.github.io) for a test drive, and for starters just prove the law of the excluded middle.

It's fun, self-contained, and yes.. marginally practical!  Partly because more academic tools like lean frequently do not have official containers =P, but mostly because this is part of a [larger demo about notebooking]({{_}}/demos/notebooking).  

!!! tip "Tip"
    This demo is a variation on many others that are [using inlined data and container-definitions]({{_}}/demos/matrioshka) and/or [working with foreign languages]({{_}}/demos/polyglots). This is an instance of [a custom containerized interpreter]({{_}}/demos/polyglots/#custom-containerized-interpreters), and also demonstrates **custom interpreter invocations**.

### Lean Demo
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

The lean demo comes in two flavors: toggle the tabs.  The **Plain Makefile** form uses `compose.mk` as a library; the **cmk-lang** form is the same logic with `compose.mk` as an interpreter, expressed in [cmk-lang]({{_}}/cmk/compiler) (which compiles to Makefile, cleaning up the syntax and clarifying intent).

